{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2167-01-21 17:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi, I'm Dr. Lee, one of the ENT doctors. You're here today for the T-tube removal and to have your trach placed back into the original stoma, correct?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2167-01-21 17:31:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, that’s right. I’ve had this T-tube for a while now, and it’s been uncomfortable. Is the procedure going to be done under anesthesia?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2167-01-21 17:32:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, it’s a brief procedure under general anesthesia. We’ll remove the T-tube, clean out the area, and place a standard trach tube back into your old stoma. It should help with comfort and airflow."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2167-01-21 17:34:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, good. I’ve been having some throat tightness lately—could that be from the T-tube?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2167-01-21 17:35:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It’s possible. The T-tube can cause irritation or pressure, especially with your history of laryngeal stenosis. We expect things to improve once it’s removed and the trach is repositioned."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2167-01-21 17:37:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I also noticed my breathing feels a bit more labored when I’m lying down. Is that normal?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2167-01-21 17:38:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Given your tracheobronchomalacia and stenosis, airway narrowing can worsen when lying flat. The new trach positioning should help stabilize your airway. We’ll monitor you closely after the procedure."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2167-01-21 17:40:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Got it. And what about pain afterward? I don’t want to be in too much discomfort."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2167-01-21 17:41:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll manage your pain with acetaminophen and, if needed, a low-dose oxycodone. Most patients feel mild soreness, but it’s usually well controlled."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2167-01-21 18:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "The procedure went smoothly. We removed the T-tube and placed the trach in the original stoma. Your airway looks stable, and the tube is secure."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2167-01-21 18:17:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s a relief. Is there any swelling to worry about?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2167-01-21 18:18:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Some mild swelling is expected, but we’ll keep an eye on it. You’re breathing comfortably now, which is a good sign."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2167-01-21 20:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Just checking in. How’s your pain level right now?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2167-01-21 20:01:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "It’s about a 3 out of 10. I took the acetaminophen earlier, and it helped."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2167-01-21 20:03:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good. We’ll keep offering it every four hours as needed. Also, your trach is clear, and your oxygen levels are stable."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2167-01-22 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ve done well overnight. Pain is controlled, and you’re breathing easily. We’re planning to discharge you this morning."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2167-01-22 09:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Great. Do I need to do anything special for trach care at home?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2167-01-22 09:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes. Keep your supplies with you—spare trach tubes, suction equipment, saline, and emergency contacts. Clean the site daily and suction as needed to keep the tube clear."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2167-01-22 09:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And if the tube gets blocked?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2167-01-22 09:07:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Use your suction catheter first. If it’s still blocked, you may need to change the tube. Always have a smaller-sized tube on hand in case the stoma is tight."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2167-01-22 09:09:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, I remember that from before. What about follow-up?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2167-01-22 09:10:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ll see ENT in the clinic in about a week. We’ll check healing and make sure the trach is functioning well."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2167-01-22 10:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your discharge meds include acetaminophen and oxycodone for pain, plus your regular fluoxetine, gabapentin, omeprazole, and albuterol nebs as needed."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2167-01-22 10:32:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ll make sure not to take extra acetaminophen with the oxycodone. I remember that from last time."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2167-01-22 10:33:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Exactly. We’ve included clear instructions. You’re doing well and ready to go home."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2167-01-22 11:00:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you, Doctor. I feel much better already."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2167-01-22 11:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. Call if you have trouble breathing, increased pain, or signs of infection. Otherwise, rest and take it easy."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2167-01-22 11:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I will. Thanks again."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2167-01-21 17:24:00",
    "admission_end": "2167-01-22 15:57:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "A young woman with a history of tracheobronchomalacia, laryngeal stenosis, and prior airway reconstructions was admitted for T-tube removal and tracheostomy replacement in the original stoma. The procedure was performed under general anesthesia and completed without complications. She was observed overnight for pain control, which was managed with acetaminophen and oxycodone as needed. Her breathing remained stable, and she demonstrated understanding of tracheostomy care. She was discharged home the following morning with instructions for trach maintenance, emergency preparedness, and follow-up with ENT. Her chronic conditions, including asthma, GERD, and bronchopulmonary dysplasia, remained stable during admission.",
    "problems": [
      "Other specified diseases of upper respiratory tract",
      "Other diseases of bronchus, not elsewhere classified",
      "Congenital subglottic stenosis",
      "Bronchopulmonary dysplasia originating in the perinatal period",
      "Unspecified asthma, uncomplicated",
      "Gastro-esophageal reflux disease without esophagitis",
      "Encounter for attention to tracheostomy",
      "Encounter for fitting and adjustment of other specified devices",
      "Anemia, unspecified"
    ]
  }
}
